  "updated": "2026-08-08T02:23:40.007359+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 128 PM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Isolated showers and storms through this weekend as \nwe settle into a typical summertime pattern. \n\nEarly afternoon water vapor imagery reveals a pair of anomalously \nstrong (nearly 600dm) H5 ridges in place (one off the east coast, \none over Utah), with an elongated trough in between them stretching \nfrom eastern Canada southwestward into Louisiana. Across NC, deep \nand broad (albeit weak) southwesterly flow remains in place from the \nsurface well into the upper levels of the troposphere. Surface obs \nand visible satellite reveal a fairly uniform atmosphere across NC \nthis afternoon with storms developing along the sea breeze along \nwith some clusters of storms along the mountains to our west. In \nbetween, conditions remain dry with temps in the mid to upper 80s as \nof 17Z. \n\nWhile still higher than normal for early August, we've eroded some \nof the PW that was in place yesterday (was nearly 1.9\", now closer \nto 1.7\"). As such, we have a bit less instability to work with this \nafternoon with MLCAPES still approaching 2000-2500 J/KG compared to \nmore than 3000 J/KG Thursday afternoon. In an atmosphere with less \nthan 20kts of bulk shear available and little in the way of pre-\nexisting surface boundaries, any storms that develop will struggle \nto sustain themselves for long and should be very isolated in \nnature. Sea breeze convection across the Sandhills/southern Coastal \nPlain is one focal area today, with storms coming off the mountains \nserving as a secondary area of attention across the western \nPiedmont. Outflow boundary collisions could spawn a few isolated \nshowers in between along the US-1 corridor but coverage should be \nspotty at best. Expect any/all storm clusters to dissipate closer to \n00Z with the loss of daytime heating. \n\nThe offshore ridge will undergo a gradual flattening process \ntomorrow through early next week, eventually building westward into \nthe Gulf and SE states by Monday. This should help to further \nsuppress deeper moisture returns into the area and overall \nconvective coverage on Saturday. Outside of weak sea breeze \nconvection, the highest chances for storms will be concentrated \nacross the western Piedmont. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Risk for hot temperatures and dangerous heat \nincrease early next week, but many failure modes exists within the \nexpected forecast pattern Wednesday onward.\n\nStrong sub-tropical ridging parked over the western Atlantic is \nexpected to be suppressed south this weekend before retrograding and \nelongated westward across FL and into the northeast Gulf into early \nnext week. Central NC is expected to situated between two bands of \nseasonably anomalous deep-layer moisture Sun through Tues, which \nshould limit widespread convective chances, although isolated \nshowers are still possible. Increasing convective debris and \nlingering small-scale forcing may couple with a band of deeper \nmoisture from the Midwest and northern Mid-Atlantic as it sags south \nand into the Carolinas Wed onward which will complicate the risk for \ncontinued dangerous heat beyond Tues. \n\nIn the absence of convection, building sub-tropical ridge to our \nsouth and warming H850 temperatures to around 20C would support low-\nmid 90s as early as Sun, and as high as upper 90s possible Tues \nthrough the remainder of the forecast period. Moisture and \nassociated heat index values during peak heating will largely depend \non the position/location of a persist Piedmont trough, with drier \ndownsloping flow to its west and more humid conditions to its east. \nLocations east of this feature, and that remain void of convection, \nwill likely reach Moderate HeatRisk and eclipse 105 degree heat \nindices during peak heating.",
